<h1>Latest news 20.04-2026</h1>

 <h2>NuTyX 26.04.3 available with cards 3.4.7</h2>
  <p>
   Spiky and me are happy to announce the new version of NuTyX 26.04.3 and cards 3.4.7 .
<h2>The short version</h2>
  <ol>
   <li>
    Remaining sysV files all removed.
   </li>
   <li>
    Installer and initrd completly review.
    They now works with busybox, systemd and a couple of extras tools.
   </li>
   <li>
    3 ISO graphics: XFCE, GNOME and KDE6.
   </li>
  </ol>
 <h2>The long version</h2>
   <p>
    Now, the distribution focuses on a single initialization system: systemd.
   <p>
    systemd 260 remove compatibility with sysv.
   <p>
    The transition from sysV to systemd is impossible.
    On the other hand, a new installation keeps your personal datas.

 <h2>Installation</h2>
  <h3>Good to know</h3>
   <p>
    The installation requires 4 Gyga bytes of RAM and a disk space of 20 GB minimum for the system.
   <p>
    The amount of disk space required for NuTyX depends on your usage.
    20 GB is a good starting point if you decide to install Flatpak. 

  <h3>Methods of installation</h3>
   <p>
    The installation can be done in bios or uefi mode. The detection is done automatically.
   <p>
    If you are lucky enough to be able to install in uefi mode, the uefi partition size can be very small.
    Attention all machines do not accept an efi partition below 50 MB (virtualbox e.g.).
   <p>
    The installation is very fast and very safe, indeed the installer will never format a partition without asking the user.
   <p>
    So if you do not replace the partition on which your data is
located, you do not fail to lose your personal data. When in doubt, make
 a backup. ;)
   <p>
    The installer works in a very simple and straightforward manner:
    However, an installation requires that the following criteria be met:
    <ol>
     <li>
      The target disk is unique and completely blank, meaning no partition table exists.
      In this case, the installer will automatically create the 3 (or 2 in BIOS) partitions required for installation.
      A confirmation message will appear asking if the user is ready to install on the created target partition.
      Once confirmed, the installation begins.
     </li>
     <li>
      A single unformatted Linux partition.
      In this case, the installer will ask to format the ONLY partition found as ext4 and will use it as the destination.
      Once confirmed, the installation begins.
     </li>
     <li>
      A single Linux partition formatted as ext4
      A confirmation message will appear asking if the user is ready to install on the target partition.
      Once confirmed, the installation begins.
     <p>
      As an added bonus, if the user keeps the same username, all personal files and folders will be preserved and restored.
     </li>
     <li>
       Multiple disks and/or multiple partitions.
      The user will select the target disk and the target partition.
      If the partition is already formatted, a confirmation message will appear asking if the user is ready to install on the target partition.
      Once confirmed, the installation begins.
      If the partition is not formatted, the installer will ask to format the selected target partition in ext4.
      Once confirmed, the installation begins.
     </li>
    </ol>
   <p>
    You have the choice between 3 installation modes:
    <ol>
     <li>
      Direct
     </li>
     <li>
      Direct from RAM
     </li>
     <li>
      For savvy users, from the live via the installation script 'install-nutyx'
     </li>
    </ol>
   <p>
    The KDE6 ISO is available on <a href="downloads">the download page.</a>

 <h2>Upgrade</h2>
   <p>
    Do the following:
   <pre class="command_user"><kbd>sudo cards upgrade
sudo shutdown -r 0</kbd></pre>

 <h2>What's new</h2>
  <p>
   glibc in 2.43, gcc 15.2.0, systemd in 260.1, curl in 8.19.0 .
  <p>
   The Xorg-server graphic server version 21.1.22 .
